# Changelog

All notable changes to this project are documented here.

## [2.1.0] - 2026-08-11

### Added

- `formatText`, `formatDateTime`, `formatCurrency` and `formatFlag` — display formatters for the
  values a table or detail page actually renders. Each takes its fallback glyph as a parameter
  (default `—`) and its locale explicitly, so they stay pure: no ambient locale, no module-level
  configuration. `formatFlag` keeps a nullish flag distinct from `false`, since "nobody answered"
  and "the answer is no" are different things to show.
- `formatFileSize` — a byte count as a person reads it (`5 MB`, `1.5 MB`, `512 KB`), trailing
  zeroes stripped, binary units by default and decimal units on request. A `unit` option pins the
  output to one unit, for a column of sizes where a per-row unit makes two numbers impossible to
  compare at a glance, or for a configured limit that should read the same whatever it is set to.
  An unrecognised `unit` falls back to the fitting one rather than rendering `NaN undefined`.
- `isAcceptedFileType` and `isWithinFileSize` — client-side file checks for an upload field, with
  `image/*` wildcard support so one list can drive both the `accept` attribute and the validation.
  Both are UX affordances rather than controls: the browser declares the type and the server must
  re-check the bytes regardless. `isWithinFileSize` treats a maximum of 0 or less as "no limit",
  so a misconfigured value accepts everything instead of rejecting everything.

  `isAcceptedFileType` compares case-insensitively by default, which is what RFC 2045 says a mime
  type is, and takes a `caseSensitive` option for mirroring a server that compares verbatim —
  accepting a file the server will then reject is a worse experience than rejecting it early.

## [2.0.0] - 2026-08-10

A rework, not a patch release. The public API changed shape, two exports are gone, and the package
is now published as a real dual ESM/CommonJS build. Read "Breaking" before upgrading.

### Breaking — packaging

- The package now ships an `exports` map. Deep imports into the build output
  (`@guebbit/js-toolkit/dist/...`) no longer resolve; import from the package root, or from a
  helper subpath such as `@guebbit/js-toolkit/getDelta`.
- The barrel has no default export. `import toolkit from '@guebbit/js-toolkit'` used to work only
  as a CommonJS-interop artifact; use `import * as toolkit` or named imports.
- `engines` now declares Node >= 20, and the build targets es2022. The package previously used
  `Array.prototype.toSorted` (es2023) with nothing declaring it, so it broke at runtime on Node 18
  with no warning at install time. That call is gone.

### Breaking — removed

- `isEmail` and `isUrl` are removed. Both were regex validators: the email pattern inherently
  rejects valid addresses and accepts invalid ones, and the URL pattern predates `URL.canParse()`
  being universally available. A validator that is confidently wrong is worse than none. Use a
  schema library, or `URL`, and confirm an address by sending mail to it.

### Breaking — behaviour

- `getDelta` returned a negative number, and with the default `size = 0` always returned
  `-|a - b|`. A distance is now non-negative. With a positive `size` the two numbers sit on a
  wrapping space and the shorter way around is returned, never more than `size / 2`.
- `getMapDistance` passed its arguments to `getDelta` in the wrong order (`(size, Xa, Xb)` against
  a `(a, b, size)` signature), mixing the map size into a coordinate. The distance between (40,70)
  and (50,50) returned `417.73`; it is now `22.36`.
- `getOverlapRange` was asymmetric for ranges sharing a start or an end:
  `getOverlapRange(0, 2, 0, 1)` gave `[0, 1]` while `getOverlapRange(0, 1, 0, 2)` gave `[0, 0]`.
  The result is now the intersection and does not depend on argument order. Ranges that merely
  touch are still not an overlap.
- `toFormData` lost every ancestor above the innermost key past two levels: `{a:{b:{c:1}}}` posted
  as `b[c]` instead of `a[b][c]`, so two sibling branches with the same inner shape collided on
  one key. The bracket chain now carries the full path.
- `timeToSeconds` returned `NaN` when called with no argument, despite defaulting to an empty
  string. Absent and blank components now count as zero; a component that is present but not a
  number still yields `NaN`, so malformed input stays visible.
- `formatNodeList` wrapped an `HTMLCollection` — which its signature accepts — as a single element
  instead of expanding it, producing a one-item array holding the collection.
- `isJson` accepts only objects and arrays and returns `Record<string, T> | T[] | false`. Its
  declared type never matched its behaviour, which returned any parsed JSON value. This also
  removes an ambiguity: `isJson('false')` parsed to the same `false` used to signal failure. Use
  `getJson` when any JSON value is acceptable.
- `getJson` and `isJson` no longer write to `console.error`. A malformed string is expected input
  for both, and the return value is the report.
- `levenshteinDistance` no longer returns a `999` sentinel when both sides are absent. Absent and
  empty are the same string, so the distance between two of them is `0`. The old value broke
  `d(x, x) === 0` and misled any caller filtering on `distance <= threshold`.

### Breaking — signatures

- `match(check, against, options)` replaces the magic-number `distance` parameter with
  `{ sensitive?, mode?, maxDistance? }`, where mode is `'exact' | 'contains' | 'contained' |
  'either' | 'fuzzy'`. The old `-1` default is now `'contained'` and `-2` is `'either'`; the
  one-way `'contains'` had no equivalent before.
- `eventDelegate` returns an unsubscribe function. It previously returned nothing while its own
  documentation claimed the listener could be removed — it could not, since the registered
  listener was an anonymous function that was never exposed. Every call leaked one.
- `getElementCenter` returns `[number, number]` instead of `number[]`.
- `ISecondsToTimeMap` has every field required instead of every field optional, so reading one no
  longer needs a non-null assertion.
- `arrayColumns` is overloaded: a bare column name returns `unknown[]`, a list of names returns
  `unknown[][]`. Both previously collapsed to `unknown[]`.
- `getValue` compares a radio's `name` as a property rather than interpolating it into a CSS
  selector, which threw on a name containing a quote or bracket.

### Added

- Real ESM and CommonJS builds from one source tree, with declarations emitted per format so both
  kinds of consumer type-check.
- Subpath exports: `@guebbit/js-toolkit/getDelta` and friends.
- `IMatchOptions` and `TMatchMode` exported from the package root, alongside `ISecondsToTimeMap`
  and `ISetCookieOptions` — the latter two were previously reachable only by importing the emitted
  declaration file directly.
- Property-based tests (fast-check) over the pure functions: round trips, idempotence, symmetry,
  the triangle inequality and per-input bounds. The `getOverlapRange` asymmetry above came from one
  of its counterexamples, now committed as a named regression case.
- Type-level tests (expect-type) over the exported signatures, gated by `tsc` — `isolatedModules`
  puts ts-jest in transpile-only mode, so a type error in a test never fails a jest run.
- Packaging smoke test (`npm run test:pack`): packs a tarball, installs it, imports it via
  `require` and `import` from both the barrel and a subpath, and type-checks an ESM and a
  CommonJS consumer.
- Per-file mutation baseline (`mutation-baseline.json`) with a gate that fails a file dropping
  below its recorded score and raises the baseline when one improves.
- A nightly workflow that re-runs the property tests on a random seed with ten times the cases,
  so the fixed per-commit seed does not stop the suite exploring.
- CI now runs the unit tests on Node 20, 22 and 24, and has jobs for the packaging test and the
  mutation baseline.
- `README.md` documenting the whole API, and `TESTING.md` documenting the test layers and what
  jsdom does not cover.

### Changed

- `npm run build` cleans `dist/` first. Six modules whose sources had been removed
  (`arrayDivide`, `matrixTranspose`, `prefersDarkScheme`, `prefersReducedMotion`, `sort`,
  `whitelist`) were still being published.
- `npm run typecheck` now type-checks the test tree as well as `src`.

### Fixed

- Three pre-existing type errors in the test suite, invisible until the tests were type-checked.
- `formatNodeList`'s empty-input test asserted nothing: jest's `toEqual` treats `[undefined]` as
  equal to `[]`.

## [1.2.3] - 2026-08-05

### Fixed

- `toFormData` — a plain `Blob` was silently dropped instead of being appended. The recursion
  guard tested `instanceof File`, so any binary value that was not a `File` (`canvas.toBlob()`,
  `fetch().blob()`, most image croppers) took the recursive branch; a `Blob` has no enumerable
  own properties, so nothing was appended and the upload went out with no file and no error.
  The guard now tests `instanceof Blob`, which covers `File` too since `File` extends `Blob`.

## [1.2.2] - 2026-07-28

### Added

- `getExecTime` — time a sync or async function's execution, resolving with its result and elapsed milliseconds
- `deleteFile` — delete a file, resolving `true`/`false` on success/absence, with an optional `onError` callback for unexpected failures
- `coerceStringArray` — coerce any value (array, comma-separated string, single value, `null`/`undefined`) into a trimmed `string[]`

## [1.2.1] - 2026-07-23

### Changed

- Hardened the test suite via mutation testing (Stryker), raising the overall mutation score from ~75% to ~90% with every source file now at or above 70%
- `secondsToTime` — removed a redundant always-true guard inside the loop and iterate with `Object.entries` (no behavior change)

## [1.2.0] - 2026-07-20

### Added

- `getUrlQueries` / `setUrlQueries` — parse and build URL query strings, framework-agnostic (works standalone or alongside any router)
- `copyToClipboard` — copy text to the clipboard, with an `execCommand` fallback for older/insecure contexts
- `downloadBlob` — trigger a client-side file download from a `Blob` or raw content
- `isInViewport` — check if an element is (fully or partially) within the viewport
- `getCookie` / `setCookie` / `deleteCookie` — read, write and remove cookies
- `prefersReducedMotion` / `prefersDarkScheme` — `matchMedia` convenience wrappers

### Removed

- `arrayDivide` — duplicate of lodash's `chunk`
- `matrixTranspose` — duplicate of lodash's `zip`
- `sort` — duplicate of lodash's `orderBy`
- `whitelist` — duplicate of lodash's `pick`
